Chapter: IdmReadLine
  cites Kernel chapter IdentityManager

 The first-boot ceremony's passphrase reader, driven by a keyboard.

 codex/test/idm-key-tests covers the cryptography under this chapter and
 found real bugs there, but it reaches none of the input path: every
 function it calls is a pure function of its arguments, and the reader
 that supplies those arguments needs a keyboard nothing could drive. This
 is the other half, and it is the half this test is about.

 `idm-read-line-bytes` loops until Enter. It read `uefi-read-key` -- the
 raw PS/2 Set-1 make code -- and compared the result against ASCII 13.
 Enter's make code is 28, so the comparison could never hold: the loop ran
 forever, and every byte it collected was a scancode rather than a
 character. The passphrase prompt could not be answered, which is what the
 skip reason "blocks waiting for keyboard input" had been reporting all
 along, in the voice of an environment limitation.

 So the assertion is that this returns at all. The `.keys` sidecar types
 A, B, Enter (make codes 30, 48, 28); the reader must yield exactly two
 bytes and stop. A regression to the raw call does not make this test
 wrong -- it makes it hang, and the harness's wall budget calls that a
 failure.

 The bytes are 97 and 98: lowercase, because nothing here pressed shift and
 `poll-key` now decodes shift. A passphrase read this way is
 case-sensitive. These two numbers were 65 and 66 -- uppercase -- before
 the shift latch landed, and the change from one to the other is exactly
 why every identity provisioned before it stops unlocking: the passphrase
 migration was chosen deliberately, not slid in. This test pins that the
 reader still returns the two typed letters and stops, in the new case.

Section: Definitions

  irl-show : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Console] Integer
  irl-show (xs) (i) (len) =
    if i >= len then 0
    else act
      print-line-uni (integer-to-text (list-at xs i))
      irl-show xs (i + 1) len
    end

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    let bs = idm-read-line-bytes
    in act
      print-line-uni (integer-to-text (list-length bs))
      irl-show bs 0 (list-length bs)
      print-line-uni "returned"
    end
  end
